            <div xml:id="d455">

                <pb facs="0522" n="418" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg418"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.00 Revolutions/388: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Morgan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-09-27T15:00:00-03:00">September 27,
                            1932—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 27—1:17 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>115. Disapprove Consul General Cameron’s recommendation in his telegram of
                    September 27, noon, regarding a United States warship which was made without
                    consulting Embassy. His Majesty’s sloop <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Scars-borough</hi> arrived Rio de Janeiro today for an ordinary official
                    visit. This probably created report in São Paulo of British measures for
                    protection. Am familiar with Department’s telegraphic instruction No. 60 of
                    October 11, 2 p.m., 1930.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d455-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Morgan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
